   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Продуктов всё равно мало, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'There is still not enough food, Chef.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'of groceries' before landing on 'little'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Надо сегодня всех гостей накормить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We need to feed all the guests today.' Mikhail brings out the contrast between 'need' and 'tonight' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Вот вам ещё свежие продукты.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Here are more fresh groceries for you.' The meaning hinges on 'here' working together with 'groceries' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "У меня есть свои каналы.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I have my own channels.' Russians say 'by me' or 'with us' where English says 'I have' or 'we have', without needing a separate word for 'have'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Витя, свадебная церемония уже начинается.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Vitya, the wedding ceremony is already starting.' Elena brings out the contrast between 'wedding' and 'ceremony' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Кто сейчас поведёт нашу невесту?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Who is going to walk our bride right now?' In Russian, the idea of 'who' flows naturally into 'bride' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Ну как, ты уже готова?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, how are you, ready now?' The meaning hinges on 'come on' working together with 'ready' in spoken Russian. Katya answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Да, наверное, я уже готова.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, I suppose I am ready now.' Notice how Russian expresses 'yes' and 'ready' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Не переживай, всё будет хорошо.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Do not worry, everything will be fine.' The grammar packs 'not' and 'well' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Я перед свадьбой тоже волновалась.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I was nervous before my wedding too.' The '-a' ending on the past-tense verb for 'to worry' marks a female speaker or subject.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Ты должна подойти к нему.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You must walk up to him.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'you' before landing on 'him'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "elena",
   "t": "Просто посмотри в его глаза.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Just look into his eyes.' The grammar packs 'simply' and 'eyes' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
